Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Five Lanes, No Common Downbeat
- Story & Context: The debut opens at 138 BPM with only kick and one lower valiha pattern. Miora’s left hand repeats a three-note figure across the four-beat bar while her right hand waits until the second cycle before entering with a five-note upper-register pattern. Elias adds low toms only where neither hand attacks, creating a groove whose strongest composite accent shifts from bar to bar even though the kick never moves. Lova replaces every fourth programmed rim with one muted frame-drum slap. Tiana enters only after sixteen bars with two clipped kabosy fifths, establishing the first clear harmonic boundary. Sarobidy then places one damped glass note at the point where all five lanes come closest to aligning. The track never breaks down. Instead, Elias shifts the high-percussion lane one eighth note later near the midpoint, causing the entire groove to feel newly tilted without changing tempo or harmony. In the final section, the upper valiha lane disappears, revealing how much phantom motion it had created around the stable kick.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Overhead view of five narrow woven raffia strips laid across a dark wooden table, each carrying a different hand-drawn rhythm pattern, cylindrical valiha crossing the lines diagonally, small glass bar and drum-machine step lights nearby. No full lyrics; sparse spoken fragments limited to “five lanes,” “do not reset,” “same kick,” and “move only one.”

Track 2: Glass Bar Four Rings Across the Rice Wall
- Story & Context: The second track runs at 134 BPM and gives Sarobidy a larger role. One prepared glass bar is left undamped while all neighboring bars are shortened with felt. Every eight bars, the long-ringing note appears over a dense rotation of shuffled hats and upper valiha plucks. Miora responds by muting her next upper-string attack, allowing the glass decay to occupy that frequency band alone. Elias keeps the kick constant but gradually rotates three low tom sounds through different offbeats. Tiana plays a kabosy triad only when the undamped glass note lands after, rather than before, the right-hand valiha pattern. Lova adds dry bass-frame-drum strokes in place of selected electronic toms, making acoustic and synthetic low percussion alternate rather than stack. The final thirty-two bars remove all glass except the single long bar, which becomes a beacon above the increasingly dense plucked grid.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Clear tuned glass bar suspended above a low stone rice-terrace wall at night, one bar ringing freely while neighboring bars are wrapped with narrow felt strips, valiha strings and raffia fibers in macro focus, distant amber house lights across the hillside. Sparse spoken fragments limited to “bar four,” “leave it ringing,” “clear the upper lane,” and “answer after decay.”

Track 3: The Pattern Returns One Eighth Late
- Story & Context: The closer starts at 140 BPM with the fastest and simplest kick pattern on the EP. The complexity comes from one procedural rule: every sixteen bars, exactly one lane restarts one eighth note later while all others continue. First the high toms shift. Then the right-hand valiha figure moves. Then Lova’s frame-drum response relocates. The harmonic center stays on one Dorian pedal for almost three minutes, while Tiana plays only two short kabosy attacks per section. Sarobidy uses tightly damped glass notes as orientation markers, always striking the original position even after another lane has shifted. By the final minute, the combined groove sounds almost unrelated to its opening configuration despite every individual pattern remaining unchanged. One by one, the shifted lanes return to their original positions. The last element to realign is Miora’s right hand, which lands exactly with the kick for a single cycle before the recording ends abruptly.
